  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA weakening trough will move across Southern California tonight into \nearly Monday. This will bring periods of light rain showers to the \ncoastal basin with the potential for a dusting of mountain snow \nabove 6000-6500 ft. Overall shower coverage will be scattered \nwith rainfall rates mostly 0.10\" per hour or less. Rainfall totals\nwill be 0.20\" or less. Highs today are expected to be similar to \nhighs yesterday. \n\nIn the wake of the passing trough, weak transitory upper level \nridging will set up over Southern California, bringing a few degrees \nof warming. A shortwave trough will move across California \nWednesday. Forecast continues to trend dry for this shortwave \npassage with only a subtle change in the temperature forecast \nbetween Tuesday and Wednesday west of the mountains. \n\nFor Thursday, guidance has a ridge over California and the \naforementioned trough to our east, with 60 percent of ensemble \nguidance showing the trough more over Texas and weaker with 22 \npercent showing the low over the Arizona/New Mexico border, and 18\npercent showing the low in northern Mexico. All of these \nsolutions indicate offshore flow, with the solution showing the \nlow over northern Mexico the most likely solution for stronger \nwinds with the low more set up for weaker winds in the other \nsolutions. Guidance from the CW3E West-WRF shows a 60-75% chance \nof a weak Santa Ana wind event, with a 25-30% chance of weak to \nmoderate Santa Ana winds. One thing we can say with confidence is \nconditions will be warmer and drier. Highs Thursday and Friday \nwill be 5 to 15 degrees above average. Friday looks to be the \nwarmest day with NBM chances of high temperatures of 80 or higher \nacross the coastal basin and low deserts at 70-90%. Chances of \nhigh temperatures of 90 or more are zero at this time. \n\nBy Saturday, it looks likely the upper level ridge will get pushed \neast by an incoming upper level trough. 60% of ensemble members \nindicate the trough moving into Northern California with the \nother 40% showing more zonal flow. The troughing solutions are \nthe ones most likely to bring us light precipitation in Southern \nCalifornia. The current forecast follows the NBM with slight \nchances of precipitation as early as Sunday with gradual cooling \nSaturday into Sunday.",
